  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "If $sin\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5}$ and ${270}^{\u2218}<\\theta <{360}^{\u2218}$ then what is the value of $cos\u2061\\theta ?$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/01f881c353d1d113e97680cce375359cedf561e69259a6b2ba1fe15097742dda.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos\u2061\\theta $ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So, $cos\u2061\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.$",
